<section xml:id="unit2-selection-coding-practice">
  <time minutes="90" />

  <title>Selection Coding Practice Exercises</title>

  <p>These are exercises mostly written by Dr. Barb Ericson in the AP CSP and Python for Everybody ebooks on Runestone. 
  </p>

 <datafile xml:id="unittesthelper" label="unittesthelper" filename="unittesthelper.py" editable="no" hide="yes">
   <pre>
testvar = 5
def contains(item, container):
    return item if item in container else None    
   </pre>
</datafile>  

    <activity label="if-email">
    <statement>
      <p>The following code checks if an input email is valid. Run the code twice to test it once with a valid email 
        and once with an invalid email.
        Change the code to check to work for other domains than "gmail.com" by just checking for @ and . in the email.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python" add-files="unittesthelper">
        <code>
email = "student@school.edu"
# Change this to check if @ is in the email and . is in the email.
if "@gmail.com" in email:
    print("Valid email.")
else:
    print("Invalid email.")
        </code>
       <tests>

from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual('"@" in email', contains('"@" in email', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for "@" in email')
        self.assertEqual('"." in email', contains('"." in email', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for "." in email')
        self.assertEqual(None, contains("@gmail.com", self.getEditorText()), "Don't use @gmail.com")

myTests().main()
</tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

  <activity label="selection-fix-bugs">
          <statement>
            <p>Fix the indentation errors in the code, and change it to use only one if/else statement. The code should print
              <q>The number is 5</q> when the number is 5, and should print <q>The number is NOT 5</q> when it is not.</p>
          </statement>
          <program interactive="activecode" language="python" xml:id="cndtnl-wc-fiveq_editor">
            <code>
x = 5
if x == 5:
print("The number is 5")
if x != 5:
print("The number is NOT 5")
        </code>
        <tests>

from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
      def test1(self):
          def contains(item, container):
              return item if item in container else None

          self.assertEqual(None, contains("if x != 5", self.getEditorText()), "Don't use x != 5")
          self.assertEqual("else", contains("else", self.getEditorText()), "Use else")

myTests().main()
</tests>
          </program>

          </activity>

     <activity label="selection-price">
    <statement>
      <p>Fix 3 indentation errors in the code below, and change it to use only one if/else statement. 
        The code below is supposed to set the price to 1.5 if the weight is less than 2 and
        otherwise set it to 1.3. It then calculates the total by multiplying the weight by the price.
     </p>
       

    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
          weight = 1
          if weight &lt; 2:
          price = 1.50
          if weight &gt;= 2:
          price = 1.30
              total = weight * price
          print(total)
        </code>
      <tests>

from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertAlmostEqual(1.5, total, 2, "Testing the total is 1.5.")
        self.assertEqual("else:", contains("else:", self.getEditorText()), "Check for else:")

myTests().main()
</tests>
    </program>
  </activity>

    <activity label="gradCredits">
    <statement>
      <p>To graduate from a particular high school, a student needs 22 or more total credits and at least 3 math
        credits. 
      Given the number of <c>totalCredits</c> earned and the number of <c>mathCredits</c>, print out 
        "Can Graduate" or "Cannot Graduate".  
      </p>

    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        totalCredits = 22
        mathCredits = 2
        # Check if the student can graduate if totalCredits is at least 22 and mathCredits is at least 3.
        # Print "Can Graduate" or "Cannot Graduate"

      </code>
      <tests>


from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual("totalCredits&gt;=22", contains("totalCredits&gt;=22", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), 'Check for totalCredits &gt;= 22')
        self.assertEqual("mathCredits&gt;=3", contains("mathCredits&gt;=3", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), 'Check for mathCredits &gt;= 3')
        self.assertEqual('else:', contains('else:', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for else:')
        self.assertEqual( "Cannot Graduate", contains("Cannot Graduate", self.getOutput()),"Output has Cannot Graduate (for 22 total and 2 math)")

myTests().main()
      </tests>
    </program>
  </activity>

  <activity label="is-even">
    <statement>
      <p>Write code to check if a number is even or odd. An even number has a 0 remainder when divided by 2. 
        Remember that you can use the modulo operator <c>%</c> to get the remainder of a division. 
        <c>number % 2</c> equals 0 if the number is even and 1 if it is odd.
       </p>

    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        number = 9
        # Check if the number is even using % and print out "even" or "odd"



      </code>
      <tests>

from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual("number%2==0", contains("number%2==0", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), "Check if number % 2 == 0")
        self.assertEqual("else:", contains("else:", self.getEditorText()), "Use else: for odd numbers")
        self.assertEqual("odd", contains("odd", self.getOutput()), "Check that the output is odd for 9")

myTests().main()
</tests>
    </program>
  </activity>

  <activity label="calculatePay">
    <statement>
      <p>Write code to calculate pay given the number of hours worked and an
        hourly pay rate. If the hours worked are less than or equal to 40, it should just return 
        the hours times the hourly pay. 
        If more than 40 hours were worked, the person should earn normal pay for 40
        hours and 1.5 times the normal pay for the hours over 40. So if someone worked 50 hours, at
        $18 per hour they would receive <m>40 \cdot 18 + ((50 - 40) \cdot 18) \cdot 1.5</m></p>

    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        hours = 50
        payRate = 18
        # If the hours are less than or equal to 40, calculate pay as hours times payRate
        # But if the hours are more than 40, calculate normal pay for 40 hours 
        # and 1.5 times the normal pay for the hours over 40.

      </code>
      <tests>


from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual("hours&lt;=40", contains("hours&lt;=40", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), "Check for hours &lt;= 40")
        self.assertEqual("hours-40", contains("hours-40", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), "Check for hours - 40")
        self.assertEqual("990", contains("990", self.getOutput()), "Answer is 990 for 50 hours at $18/hr + overtime")

myTests().main()
</tests>
    </program>
  </activity>

  <activity label="getClassFromCredits">
    <statement>
      <p>Write code to determine the class level based on the number of credits someone has taken. A
        Senior has taken 90 or more credits. A Junior hasn’t taken that many credits, but has taken at
        least 60. A Sophomore has taken at least 30 credits. A First Year is anyone who has taken 30
        or less.</p>
      <p>Write code to to figure out the class level based on the number of credits. Your code should use 
        <c>credits</c> and the above logic to print:
        <c>"Senior"</c>, <c>"Junior"</c>, <c>"Sophomore"</c>, or <c>"First Year"</c>.</p>

    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        credits = 40
        # Write if statements to determine if the student is a Senior, Junior, Sophomore or First Year


      </code>
      <tests>


from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual("credits&gt;=90", contains("credits&gt;=90", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), "Check for credits &gt;= 90")
        self.assertEqual("credits&gt;=60", contains("credits&gt;=60", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), "Check for credits &gt;= 60")
        self.assertEqual("credits&gt;=30", contains("credits&gt;=30", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")), "Check for credits &gt;= 30")
        self.assertEqual("Sophomore", contains("Sophomore", self.getOutput()), "Output has Sophomore (for 40 credits)")

myTests().main()
</tests>
    </program>
  </activity>


  <activity label="mealCost">
    <statement>
      <p>The program below is supposed to calculate the final bill for a meal at a fast-food restaurant in a 
        state where meals that are taken to go are not taxed, but meals eaten in the restaurant are.</p>
      <p>Your job is to calculate the total cost given the initial <c>mealPrice</c> and the <c>mealLocation</c>. 
        If <c>mealLocation</c> has the string <c>"to go"</c> anywhere in it, the total cost should be
        just the meal price. Otherwise, it should be the price + 8% (1.08 times the meal price).</p>
      <p>Remember that you can use <c>in</c> to check if one string appears inside another.</p>

    </statement>
    <program  interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        mealPrice = 10
        mealLocation = "I want it to dine in please"
        # if the mealLocation has "to go" in it, then the cost is just the mealPrice
        # otherwise the cost is the mealPrice times 1.08 (for the tax)
        

      </code>
      <tests>

from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual('"to go" in mealLocation:', contains('"to go" in mealLocation:', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for "to go" in mealLocation:')
        self.assertEqual('else', contains('else', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for else')
        self.assertEqual("10.8", contains("10.8", self.getOutput()), "Output has cost 10.8 (with tax)")

myTests().main()
</tests>
    </program>
  </activity>

  <activity label="museumTicket">
    <statement>
      <p>The program below is supposed to figure out the cost of a ticket for a customer to a museum. Anyone who is at least
        65 or who is younger than 12 gets in free. Anyone who is 12 through 17 gets in for $8. Everyone else is $15.</p>
    </statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
        age = 12
        # Cost is 0 for 65+ or under 12, $8 for 12-17, of $15 otherwise
        

  
      </code>
      <tests>


from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def testOne(self):
        def contains(item, container):
            return item if item in container else None

        self.assertEqual("age&gt;=65", contains("age&gt;=65", self.getEditorText().replace(' ', '')), 'Check for age &gt;= 65')
        self.assertEqual('if', contains('if', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for if')
        self.assertEqual("elif", contains("elif", self.getEditorText().replace(' ', '')), 'Check for elif')
        self.assertEqual('else', contains('else', self.getEditorText()), 'Check for else')
        self.assertEqual("8", contains("8", self.getOutput()), "Output has cost 8 (for 12-17)")

myTests().main()
</tests>
    </program>
  </activity>



  </section>